    "https://careers.ucalgary.ca/jobs/18210493-assistant-professor-department-of-political-science-canada-impact-plus-emerging-leader": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Department of Political Science (Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Department of Political Science (Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader) in \n            \n                Calgary,\n            \n            \n                \n                \n                    AB,\n                \n            \n            Canada Skip to main content Menu Careers Home See All Jobs Candidate Information Total Rewards at UCalgary Workplace Diversity and Inclusion Indigenous Candidates Accessibility and Accommodation Application FAQ's Apply Now Require Authentication must be enabled to output referral button. Assistant Professor, Department of Political Science (Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader) Job ID: 38523 Location: Main Campus Updated September 04, 2026 Apply Now Share: Share on Twitter share to twitter Share on Facebook share to facebook Share on LinkedIn share to linkedin Share with Email Loading job Back to Search Results Previous Opportunity Next Opportunity We would like to take this opportunity to acknowledge the traditional territories of the people of the Treaty 7 region in Southern Alberta. The City of Calgary is also home to Métis Nation within Alberta (Nose Hill Métis District 5 and Elbow Métis District 6). Description The University of Calgary invites applications for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor in the Department of Political Science , in the area of intelligence and national security, with a particular focus on open-source intelligence. The start date for the appointment is on or before July 1, 2027. This position is linked to the University's successful Canada Impact+ Research Chair initiative, particularly the Eddie Goldenberg Research Chair of Canada in Intelligence and Security in Changing Global Order, residing in the Centre of Military, Security and Strategic Studies (CMSS) at the University of Calgary, a global hub of research excellence on national security issues. The successful candidate for the Emerging Leader will conduct innovative, high-impact research, secure external funding, build partnerships, contribute to teaching and curriculum development, and advance knowledge mobilization, innovation, commercialization, policy, and broader societal impact. Candidates should demonstrate research excellence, foster collaborative approaches, and a commitment to fostering inclusive research and learning environments. We seek an outstanding early career researcher with a PhD or equivalent in Political Science or related discipline, as required by the University of Calgary, and who specializes in intelligence and international security studies who will establish an independent research program aligned with the strategic priority area, and conduct transdisciplinary research in collaboration with, but not limited to, the Eddie Goldenberg Research Chair of Canada in Intelligence and Security in Changing Global Order. Proven success in research and teaching in intelligence studies is essential. The successful candidate will have a track record of policy-relevant research and engagement with political and policy stakeholders in both the private and public sectors involved with intelligence and security policy. Candidates with specific experience and expertise in the areas of international intelligence cooperation, open-source intelligence analysis with specific reference to large qualitative data sets, and public attitudes toward security and intelligence, are particularly encouraged to apply. Expertise within arctic security would be an asset, though not required for success. Research Area Intelligence and security studies are an increasingly important component of contemporary strategic studies. The field examines how governments and other organizations collect, assess, interpret, and use information to understand threats, manage uncertainty, and inform strategic and operational decision-making. While traditionally associated with classified intelligence and state intelligence agencies, the rapid growth of publicly available information, digital technologies, artificial intelligence, and open-source intelligence (OSINT) has fundamentally expanded the field. Governments, private organizations, researchers, and non-state actors can now collect and analyse vast quantities of information to identify emerging threats, understand adversary behaviour, and generate timely assessments of the security environment. The Emerging Leader in intelligence and national security position will strengthen CMSS expertise in intelligence and security studies while complementing the Centre's existing and developing research strengths in diverse facets of security studies such as the arctic region, energy, conventional versus asymmetrical warfare, state versus non-governmental participants, and human security. Particular emphasis would be placed on open-source intelligence, emerging intelligence technologies, information analysis, and the application of these capabilities to contemporary security challenges. This position will support research examining how governments and other actors can exploit publicly available information to improve situational awareness, identify emerging threats, and understand activities that may otherwise remain difficult to observe. Working alongside senior scholars in intelligence studies, including the Centre's developing expertise in intelligence and security, the position would help translate these capabilities into sustained research, graduate training, collaborative projects, and policy engagement. The successful candidate would therefore contribute not only to the study of intelligence and security but also to CMSS's broader objective of developing innovative, interdisciplinary approaches to understanding emerging strategic challenges facing Canada and its allies. The Emerging Leader will work in collaboration with the Eddie Goldenberg Research Chair of Canada in Intelligence and Security in a Changing Global Order . The Chair will explore the role of intelligence and its role in Canada's national security policy in the new geo-political order that is emerging out of the post-Cold War `rules-based order'. The Chair will conduct world-leading transdisciplinary research into Canada's intelligence and security policy. It will also build strong relationships with Canada's foreign and defence policy communities to ensure program research reaches political and policy stakeholders. Finally, it will develop research capacity and wider expertise in intelligence and security issues through the creation of national and international networks of expertise around a multidisciplinary team of researchers at the University of Calgary. The Emerging Leader will be able to provide pivotal contributions to the core aims of the research program as well as to the specific workstreams related to an Open-Source Arctic Security Observation Unit and Public Attitudes and Expectations toward Security and Intelligence in Canada . The Emerging Leader will have a growing reputation in the multidisciplinary field of intelligence studies. A strong portfolio of first-rate published work on contemporary security and intelligence issues would be an asset. Expertise on issues related to international intelligence cooperation, on the acquisition and exploitation of open-source intelligence and on new challenges facing policy-makers in the wider security environment would be particularly valuable. Experience engaging with high-level political and policy organizations and providing evidence to parliamentary bodies would be complementary. If the candidate had experience in communicating their research to the media and wider public, that would be an enormous benefit. These skills and experience would not only complement the skills and experience of the Chair but also add new areas of strength to the research program as a whole. The successful candidate will be expected to: Contribute to the research and societal impact objectives of the Eddie Goldenberg Research Chair of Canada in Intelligence and Security in a Changing Global Order. Develop a research program designed to maximize policy engagement and societal impact in the wider field of contemporary intelligence and security studies. Develop and sustain an independent, externally funded research programme leading to peer-reviewed publication of national and international standing; Teach effectively at both undergraduate and graduate levels, including  courses in the candidate's area of specialisation, across a variety of formats, settings and modalities. Supervise and mentor graduate students at the MA and PhD levels. Contribute to the collegial governance of the Department, the Faculty of Arts, the University, and to the wider scholarly and public community. Eligibility In accordance with the Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders program requirements : Candidates must be internationally based at the time of recruitment (both working and residing outside Canada). Successful candidates will have up until July 1, 2027 to start in the role. Expatriate Canadians wishing to relocate to Canada are welcome to apply. An Early Career Researcher is a researcher within five years from the date of their first research-related appointment, minus eligible delays in research, where: research-related appointments are defined as those where the individual has the autonomy to conduct research independently; and all eligible leaves (e.g., maternity, parental, medical, bereavement) are credited as twice the amount of time taken, and professional leaves (e.g., training, sabbatical, administrative) are not credited. More information at canada.ca To Apply Interested individuals are encouraged to apply online via the University of Calgary Careers site using the `Apply Now' link. Complete applications should include: Cover letter and curriculum vitae, including the name and contact information of three referees. Statement of research interests, accomplishments and vision (maximum 2 page) A teaching statement, including an articulation of the candidate's teaching philosophy, approach to course design at the undergraduate and graduate level, and approach to research supervision (up to 2 pages). Statement on Equity, Diversity and Inclusion (maximum 1 pages) Application Deadline: October 30, 2026 If you have questions about this opportunity, please contact Dr. Robert Huebert, rhuebert@ucalgary.ca . The University of Calgary has launched an institution-wide Indigenous Strategy ii' taa'poh'to'p committing to creating a rich, vibrant, and culturally competent campus that welcomes and supports Indigenous Peoples, encourages Indigenous community partnerships, is inclusive of Indigenous perspectives in all that we do. As an equitable and inclusive employer, the University of Calgary recognizes that a diverse staff/faculty benefits and enriches the work, learning and research experiences of the entire campus and greater community. We are committed to removing barriers that have been historically encountered by some people in our society. We strive to recruit individuals who will further enhance our diversity and will support their academic and professional success while they are here. In particular, we encourage members of the designated groups (women, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities, members of visible/racialized minorities, and diverse sexual orientation and gender identities) to apply. To ensure a fair and equitable assessment, we offer accommodation at any stage during the recruitment process to applicants with disabilities. Questions regarding diversity at UCalgary can be sent to the Office of Institutional Commitments ( oicteam@ucalgary.ca ) and requests for accommodations can be sent to Carley L'Hirondelle, People & Culture ( hrhire@ucalgary.ca ). The University of Calgary adheres to the principles of the San Francisco Declaration on Research Assessment (DORA) and strives to evaluate research impact responsibly and inclusively by explicitly considering discipline-appropriate methods for demonstrating the quality, significance, and societal impact of a broad range of research contributions. The University of Calgary recognizes that candidates have varying career paths and that career interruptions can be part of an excellent academic record. Candidates are encouraged but not required to provide any relevant information about their experience and/or career interruptions to allow for a fair assessment of their application. Selection committees have been instructed to give careful consideration to, and be sensitive to the impact of, career interruptions when assessing the candidate's research productivity. The University of Calgary's comprehensive benefits and pension program is designed to promote a productive level of health and well-being to staff members. To learn about our comprehensive benefits package, please visit: https://www.ucalgary.ca/hr/benefits-pension/perks/benefits-ucalgary . The terms and conditions of this appointment, including salary, are governed by the collective agreement between the Faculty Association of the University of Calgary and the Governors of the University of Calgary. Detailed information is available here . About the University of Calgary UCalgary is Canada's entrepreneurial university, located in Canada's most enterprising city. It is a top research university and one of the highest-ranked universities of its age. Founded in 1966, its 36,000 students experience an innovative learning environment, made rich by research, hands-on experiences and entrepreneurial thinking. It is Canada's leader in the creation of start-ups . Start something today at the University of Calgary. For more information, visit ucalgary.ca . The university's commitment to the Indigenous Strategy is evident through the oversight of 18 Elders serving on the Circle of Advisors and the many unit-based circles working towards implementation of the strategy including the Faculty Advisory Circle and teams such as the Office of Indigenous Engagement, the Writing Symbols Lodge, and the Indigenous Research Support Team. Many Indigenous-based events and processes are impacting curriculum, programming and polices at the University of Calgary. About Calgary, Alberta Calgary is one of the world's cleanest cities and has been named one of the world's most livable cities for years. Calgary is a city of leaders - in business, community, philanthropy and volunteerism. Calgarians benefit from a growing number of world-class dining and cultural events and enjoy more days of sunshine per year than any other major Canadian city. Calgary is less than an hour's drive from the majestic Rocky Mountains and boasts the most extensive urban pathway and bikeway network in North America. With a growing urban Indigenous population of approximately 50,000 people, there are several Indigenous organizations and many events and ceremonies taking place in the city throughout the year. Posting Date: September 4, 2026 Closing Date: October 30, 2026 Share: Share with Email Share on Twitter share to twitter Share on Facebook share to facebook Share on LinkedIn share to linkedin Apply Now facebook twitter linkedin instagram youtube Digital Privacy Statement Privacy Policy Website Feedback University of Calgary 2500 University Drive NW Calgary Alberta T2N 1N4 CANADA Copyright © 2026 The University of Calgary, located in the heart of Southern Alberta, both acknowledges and pays tribute to the traditional territories of the peoples of Treaty 7, which include the Blackfoot Confederacy (comprised of the Siksika, the Piikani, and the Kainai First Nations), the Tsuut’ina First Nation, and the Stoney Nakoda (including Chiniki, Bearspaw, and Goodstoney First Nations). The City of Calgary is also home to Métis Nation within Alberta (Nose Hill Métis District 5 and Elbow Métis District 6). The University of Calgary is situated on land Northwest of where the Bow River meets the Elbow River, a site traditionally known as Moh’kins’tsis to the Blackfoot, Wîchîspa to the Stoney Nakoda, and Guts’ists’i to the Tsuut’ina. On this land and in this place we strive to learn together, walk together, and grow together \"in a good way.\""
    },
    "https://careers.ucalgary.ca/jobs/18239560-assistant-professor-materials-interfaces-and-fabrication-for-quantum-technologies-canada-impact-plus-emerging-leader": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Materials, Interfaces, and Fabrication for Quantum Technologies (Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Materials, Interfaces, and Fabrication for Quantum Technologies (Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader) in \n            \n                Calgary,\n            \n            \n                \n                \n                    AB,\n                \n            \n            Canada Skip to main content Menu Careers Home See All Jobs Candidate Information Total Rewards at UCalgary Workplace Diversity and Inclusion Indigenous Candidates Accessibility and Accommodation Application FAQ's Apply Now Require Authentication must be enabled to output referral button. Assistant Professor, Materials, Interfaces, and Fabrication for Quantum Technologies (Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader) Job ID: 38597 Location: Main Campus Updated September 14, 2026 Apply Now Share: Share on Twitter share to twitter Share on Facebook share to facebook Share on LinkedIn share to linkedin Share with Email Loading job Back to Search Results Previous Opportunity Next Opportunity We would like to take this opportunity to acknowledge the traditional territories of the people of the Treaty 7 region in Southern Alberta. The City of Calgary is also home to Métis Nation within Alberta (Nose Hill Métis District 5 and Elbow Métis District 6). Description The University of Calgary invites applications for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor either in the Faculty of Science or Schulich School of Engineering, in the area of Materials, Interfaces, and Fabrication for Quantum Technologies .  The start date for the appointment is on or before July 1, 2027. This position is linked to the University's successful Canada Impact+ Research Chair initiative, particularly the Eddie Goldenberg Research Chair of Canada in Scalable Quantum Network Infrastructure , residing in the Faculty of Science at the University of Calgary. The successful candidate for the Emerging Leader will conduct innovative, high-impact research, secure external funding, build partnerships, contribute to teaching and curriculum development, and advance knowledge mobilization, innovation, commercialization, policy, and broader societal impact. Candidates should demonstrate research excellence, foster collaborative approaches, and a commitment to fostering inclusive research and learning environments. Research Area: Building on UCalgary's established strengths across the quantum science and technology spectrum - particularly in quantum interfaces, devices, and sensing - the University is seeking candidates for an IMPACT+ Emerging Leader faculty position in Materials, Interfaces, and Fabrication for Quantum Technologies. This position offers an opportunity to expand UCalgary's research capacity in materials science for quantum technologies. The University of Calgary requires that candidates have completed a doctoral degree and have expertise in one or more of the following areas: Materials science for quantum technologies Materials engineering for quantum technologies Computational materials research for quantum technologies The successful candidate will develop an innovative research program focused on advancing the design, development, and fabrication of materials for quantum technologies. Relevant areas of research may include diamond growth and the development or characterization of other quantum materials. The candidate's research is expected to leverage the new capabilities provided by UCalgary's qLab infrastructure, scheduled to open in 2027, and/or the University's growing high-performance computing capacity for quantum-related research. The position may involve experimental, computational, or integrated approaches and will be aligned with one of the Departments of Chemistry, Computer Science, or Electrical and Software Engineering. The successful candidate will be expected to: Contribute to the research and societal impact objectives of the Eddie Goldenberg Research Chair of Canada in Scalable Quantum Network Infrastructure . Develop a research program designed on improving and expanding the design and fabrication of quantum materials through research focuses such as diamond or other quantum material growth. Develop and sustain an independent, externally funded research programme leading to peer-reviewed publication of national and international standing. Teach effectively at both undergraduate and graduate levels, including courses in the candidate's area of specialization, across a variety of formats, settings and modalities. Supervise and mentor graduate students at the MSc and PhD levels. Eligibility In accordance with the Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders program requirements : Candidates must be internationally based at the time of recruitment (both working and residing outside Canada). Successful candidates will have up until July 1, 2027 to start in the role. Expatriate Canadians wishing to relocate to Canada are welcome to apply. An Early Career Researcher is a researcher within five years from the date of their first research-related appointment, minus eligible delays in research, where: research-related appointments are defined as those where the individual has the autonomy to conduct research independently; and all eligible leaves (e.g., maternity, parental, medical, bereavement) are credited as twice the amount of time taken, and professional leaves (e.g., training, sabbatical, administrative) are not credited. More information at canada.ca To Apply Interested individuals are encouraged to apply online via the University of Calgary Careers site using the `Apply Now' link. Complete applications should include: Cover letter and curriculum vitae, including the name and contact information of three referees. Statement of research interests, accomplishments and vision (maximum 2 page) A teaching statement, including an articulation of the candidate's teaching philosophy, approach to course design at the undergraduate and graduate level, and approach to research supervision (up to 2 pages). Statement on Equity, Diversity and Inclusion (maximum 1 pages) Application Deadline: November 13, 2026 If you have questions about this opportunity, please contact scidean@ucalgary.ca The University of Calgary has launched an institution-wide Indigenous Strategy ii' taa'poh'to'p committing to creating a rich, vibrant, and culturally competent campus that welcomes and supports Indigenous Peoples, encourages Indigenous community partnerships, is inclusive of Indigenous perspectives in all that we do. As an equitable and inclusive employer, the University of Calgary recognizes that a diverse staff/faculty benefits and enriches the work, learning and research experiences of the entire campus and greater community. We are committed to removing barriers that have been historically encountered by some people in our society. We strive to recruit individuals who will further enhance our diversity and will support their academic and professional success while they are here. In particular, we encourage members of the designated groups (women, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities, members of visible/racialized minorities, and diverse sexual orientation and gender identities) to apply. To ensure a fair and equitable assessment, we offer accommodation at any stage during the recruitment process to applicants with disabilities. Questions regarding [diversity] EDI at UCalgary can be sent to the Office of Institutional Commitments ( oicteam@ucalgary.ca ) and requests for accommodations can be sent to Carley L'Hirondelle, People & Culture ( hrhire@ucalgary.ca ). The terms and conditions of this appointment, including salary, are governed by the collective agreement between the Faculty Association of the University of Calgary and the Governors of the University of Calgary. Detailed information is available here . The University of Calgary adheres to the principles of the San Francisco Declaration on Research Assessment (DORA) and strives to evaluate research impact responsibly and inclusively by explicitly considering discipline-appropriate methods for demonstrating the quality, significance, and societal impact of a broad range of research contributions. The University of Calgary recognizes that candidates have varying career paths and that career interruptions can be part of an excellent academic record. Candidates are encouraged but not required to provide any relevant information about their experience and/or career interruptions to allow for a fair assessment of their application. Selection committees have been instructed to give careful consideration to, and be sensitive to the impact of, career interruptions when assessing the candidate's research productivity. The University of Calgary's comprehensive benefits and pension program is designed to promote a productive level of health and well-being to staff members. To learn about our comprehensive benefits package, please visit: https://www.ucalgary.ca/hr/benefits-pension/perks/benefits-ucalgary. About Quantum at the University of Canada UCalgary¿s quantum research and innovation ecosystem is a dynamic collection of interconnected initiatives and programs that support quantum research from foundational science through technology integration with industry. It includes: Quantum City, where quantum technology becomes quantum solutions; Quantum Horizons Alberta focused on quantum foundations; the Canada Excellence Research Chair in Photonic Quantum Science and Technologies; qHub (collision space) and qLab (advanced fabrication and characterization facility); and training initiatives IQuCode CREATE and the globally-unique Professional Master of Quantum Computing. About the University of Calgary UCalgary is Canada's entrepreneurial university, located in Canada's most enterprising city. It is a top research university and one of the highest-ranked universities of its age. Founded in 1966, its 36,000 students experience an innovative learning environment, made rich by research, hands-on experiences and entrepreneurial thinking. It is Canada's leader in the creation of start-ups . Start something today at the University of Calgary. For more information, visit ucalgary.ca . The university's commitment to the Indigenous Strategy is evident through the oversight of 18 Elders serving on the Circle of Advisors and the many unit-based circles working towards implementation of the strategy including the Faculty Advisory Circle and teams such as the Office of Indigenous Engagement, the Writing Symbols Lodge, and the Indigenous Research Support Team. Many Indigenous-based events and processes are impacting curriculum, programming and polices at the University of Calgary. About Calgary, Alberta Calgary is one of the world's cleanest cities and has been named one of the world's most livable cities for years. Calgary is a city of leaders - in business, community, philanthropy and volunteerism. Calgarians benefit from a growing number of world-class dining and cultural events and enjoy more days of sunshine per year than any other major Canadian city. Calgary is less than an hour's drive from the majestic Rocky Mountains and boasts the most extensive urban pathway and bikeway network in North America. With a growing urban Indigenous population of approximately 50,000 people, there are several Indigenous organizations and many events and ceremonies taking place in the city throughout the year. Posting Date: September 14, 2026 Closing Date: November 13, 2026 Share: Share with Email Share on Twitter share to twitter Share on Facebook share to facebook Share on LinkedIn share to linkedin Apply Now facebook twitter linkedin instagram youtube Digital Privacy Statement Privacy Policy Website Feedback University of Calgary 2500 University Drive NW Calgary Alberta T2N 1N4 CANADA Copyright © 2026 The University of Calgary, located in the heart of Southern Alberta, both acknowledges and pays tribute to the traditional territories of the peoples of Treaty 7, which include the Blackfoot Confederacy (comprised of the Siksika, the Piikani, and the Kainai First Nations), the Tsuut’ina First Nation, and the Stoney Nakoda (including Chiniki, Bearspaw, and Goodstoney First Nations). The City of Calgary is also home to Métis Nation within Alberta (Nose Hill Métis District 5 and Elbow Métis District 6). The University of Calgary is situated on land Northwest of where the Bow River meets the Elbow River, a site traditionally known as Moh’kins’tsis to the Blackfoot, Wîchîspa to the Stoney Nakoda, and Guts’ists’i to the Tsuut’ina. On this land and in this place we strive to learn together, walk together, and grow together \"in a good way.\""
